xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.6) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * NOT RELEASED.  This stanza describes the tree since the `v0.5` tag.  Its
    stamp is what makes two builds of it agree on bytes, not a claim that
    anyone published it; the release commit replaces `UNRELEASED` with the
    record of what was tagged.

  * The layout editor can add a row, remove one, and change which row is on
    top.  Four controls sit under the fields - `add`, `remove`, `up`,
    `down` - and each entry of the add menu says, before it is pressed,
    which row the new one copies.  A new row is always a copy of a row that
    already draws: this layout's own row of that kind if it has one,
    otherwise a real row from the 28 layouts the editor opens, with this
    repo's five exports ranked first because every row in them is carried to
    a zero-pixel diff against the picture it exports.  No default table was
    written, because an honest one is not available: the vendor's
    application bundles are not in this tree - only their file names are, in
    PROTOCOL.md section 10 - so the files are the only evidence of what a
    row of a kind looks like.

  * What that evidence says, re-counted from the files on every run rather
    than trusted from a page: all 185 rows in the 28 layouts carry exactly
    the five keys `id`, `layerIndex`, `type`, `property`, `widgetContent`,
    `property` is a one-element list in every one of them, and
    `widgetContent` is `""` in all of them.  A new row carries the
    7 / 7 / 11 / 14 / 7 / 9 keys that `text` / `data` / `bar` / `arc` /
    `special` / `line` rows always carry, and every one of its values is a
    value rows of that kind already use.  A `special` row is refused without
    a name from the five drawings `themes.SPECIALS` registers, because a
    `special` nothing draws is no picture rather than a thinner one.  Ids
    follow the spelling of the file being edited - UUIDs in the vendor's,
    `xigma-...` in ours - and stay unique inside a layout, which
    `themes.validate` asks only softly (the vendor's 152 rows in 27 files
    answer to 54 distinct ids, one id shared by 18 of those rows) and the
    model obeys it anyway.

  * Where a new row lands is decided by the painter's own boxes.  A trial
    draw with the row in it reports the box that row actually paints - an
    anchor does not mean the same thing for every kind and every
    `xigma.anchor` - and that box is walked over the canvas in 4 px steps to
    the nearest free position.  Where there is no such position the row
    stays where its source sits and the sentence says so out loud; the row
    is left selected so the reader can drag it.  Quietly moving a row nobody
    asked to move, and parking one out of sight without saying, are both the
    silent repair this model refuses elsewhere.  How an add ends follows
    this machine's faces, so the counts are labelled pairs: the 159 adds
    across the 28 layouts come out 35 / 109 / 15 - clear where their source
    sits, moved to the nearest free spot, still meeting a box - on the
    author's bench, and 35 / 109 / 15 on GitHub's runner.  The reorder pair
    differs by two rows' worth of ink: 120 adjacent pairs whose boxes never
    meet here and 118 there, and of the 12 whose boxes do meet, 6 moved ink
    and 6 did not, on either machine.

  * A reorder moves no ink: the order of `osd.widget` is the draw order, and
    what a swap does is proved with pixels in both directions.  Over every
    adjacent pair of drawn rows in all 28 layouts, a swap changes the frame
    exactly where the two rows both cover some pixel opaquely and in
    different colours.  Pairs whose bounding boxes never meet must swap 0 px,
    and the gate fails if one of them moves a pixel; pairs whose boxes meet
    but whose ink never both lie opaque in the overlap also swap 0 px, which
    is what an `arc`'s box - mostly the hole its ring is drawn around - and
    two vendor `line` rows crossing in sub-pixel coverage look like.
    `layerIndex` is left exactly where it was: over the vendor's files it
    equals the row's position in 23 of their 123 rows and differs in 100,
    nothing in `layout.py` reads it, and inventing a meaning the files do not
    show would be a repair rather than a fix.

  * The last row of a layout cannot be removed, in the library's own words:
    the model empties a copy of the layout, asks `themes.validate` what that
    costs, and repeats its answer - "an `osd.widget` list with nothing in
    it: there is nothing to draw, which is not the same as an empty canvas"
    - rather than paraphrasing it.  `remove` stays disabled at one row, so a
    reader never meets that sentence for the first time at save.  Every one
    of the four acts is exactly one undo step, and the steps are
    last-in-first-out: one press after a remove puts the row back with the
    reorder still standing, the next takes the reorder back, and the frame
    says which of the two was undone.  A refusal - a kind nothing draws, an
    unnamed `special`, a move past either end, the last row - leaves
    `dirty`, the undo count and the file exactly where they were found, and
    the suite checks that rather than hoping.

  * One digit this cycle wrote down wrong, and the gate returned it before
    it shipped.  The sentence was "those 23 files' 123 rows answer to 54
    distinct ids": the 54 belongs to the whole vendor library, across 27
    files and 152 rows, while the 23 layouts this editor can open hold 123
    rows and 25 ids.  Both figures are counted in the run now and held
    against the page, so the pair cannot drift apart again.

  * A duplicated sentence in docs/gui.md - the two lines about
    `xigma.showIf` getting the same menu as `source`, printed twice in one
    bullet - went out with 0.5 and is gone.  It was found while putting the
    four bullets about structural editing into that list, which is what
    reading a page cover to cover does that reading a diff does not.

  * The window's sizing figures move, in height only.  An `arc`'s 18 field
    rows stood 796 px tall in one column and 403 in three on the author's
    bench at 0.5, 827 and 434 after the row of row-level controls, and 858 and
    465 after the name box; GitHub's runner read 723 and 378, then 751 and
    406, then 779 and 434.  So each of those two rows costs 31 px of height
    here and 28 px there, and no column's width - and the grid's own ask does
    not move at all (171 / 344 / 512 px here, 180 / 361 / 533 there), because
    the grid of rows is the grid that was already there.  The pair gate is what
    found this: the first run of this tree failed on the bench at the gate the
    suite calls `the arc box heights`, a later one failed on the runner at the
    same gate, and each failure printed that machine's own pair.  The three
    columns' floors, the controls floor by columns of rows, the fresh split,
    the narrowest column and the window's own minimum are unchanged on both
    machines, and the suite still fails any pane that has to scroll sideways to
    be read.

  * lintian has been printing one warning that no CI run has ever seen,
    because the CI job builds binary-only (`-us -uc -b`) while a source
    package build reaches the source checks:
    `missing-license-paragraph-in-dep5-copyright gpl-3.0-or-later+other
    [debian/copyright:37]`, which makes `--fail-on error,warning` exit 2.
    The `docs/previews/*` stanza names that combined licence - our drawing
    over one frame of a vendor clip - and no paragraph of that name existed
    to answer for it.  One does now, and it says what is true: GPL covers
    our half of those files and nothing else, and the combination is
    redistributed on the assessment the OTHER paragraph already states.
    Checked both ways on this bench: `dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc -d -i -I`
    at `v0.5` plus lintian on the `.dsc` answers that warning and exit 2;
    the same build with the paragraph added answers 0 errors, 0 warnings.

  * A row's edge resizes it, and the drag writes the size keys the kind
    actually owns. The pane paints eight small squares on the selected row's
    box, and `edit.Editor.size_edges` decides which of them exist by putting
    every edge through the model's own resize door, so a handle the model
    would refuse is never offered. All eight on a `bar`, whose box is `x,y`
    to `x+width,y+height`. All eight on a `line`, the one kind decided
    without drawing: `layout.line_rect` predicts the drawn box of 4 of its 4
    rows exactly, and on a row turned 90 degrees the horizontal extent comes
    from `height` - which is also why `x`/`y` are corrected from that
    prediction when the rect's own pivot, or a `lineWidth` recentring the
    thickness, puts the box somewhere other than where the drag left it. All
    eight on an `arc`, scaling the ring with its box, because `radius` and
    the stroke's `width` move the ink and leave the box where it was (a +8
    px push on `radius` moved the box 0 px and the picture by thousands of
    pixels), and keeping `sizeWidth == 2*radius + 2*width` - the arithmetic
    6 of the 6 arc rows in the library obey, all 6 of them square, so a one-
    axis drag says the box is no longer square rather than squaring it back.
    Six on a `text` or `data` row, which owns no width: not one of the 145
    carries a `width` or `height` key, so that drag searches faces - bracket
    the height, then bisect, drawing every candidate - and quotes the box
    that reached instead of the one that was asked. Four on a `special`,
    because its two axes are two different keys: `width` is the pitch
    between the figures, `fontSize` their face, and a corner has no meaning
    there.

  * Two keys no handle writes, measured rather than listed. `cornerRadius`
    is carried by 25 of the `bar`/`arc` rows and pushing it +8 left every
    box those rows have exactly where it was; `rotate` is carried by 12 `bar`
    rows and changed 0 px on all 12 of them, because nothing in `layout.py`
    reads it on a `bar`. Where a cap owns the extent, the drag is refused in
    that cap's name - `xigma.autoFitMax` fits a row to a width,
    `xigma.fitArc` fits a `special`'s figures into a ring - and the sentence
    says which of the two it is, and that the row to change is that one. A
    row that draws no box at this fixture has no edge to grab, and the
    reason comes from the painter rather than from a paraphrase of it.

  * The pane promises the ask; the model returns the result. While the mouse
    is down the pane paints, in a solid line beside the dashed box that is,
    the box `edit.Editor.resize_box` says the release would ask for - the
    same door `resize_row` answers through, so no second copy of that
    arithmetic lives in the window. A repaint in the middle of a drag (the
    note line growing, the splitter refitting the pane) puts that ask back
    rather than dropping it. A square belongs to the selected row even under
    another row's ink, because "the later row wins an overlap" is a rule
    about ink, and the note says whose ink is lying under the square. One
    press, drag and release is exactly one undo step, moves pixels, and
    writes no file: every accepted drag the suite drove put the layout back
    byte for byte under one press of undo, and every refusal left `dirty`,
    the undo count and the picture where they were found.

  * A copy can be renamed, and one layout can be saved as a second copy
    under a new name. Two buttons over a name box, `themes.rename_copy` and
    `edit.Editor.save_as` behind them, and one rule shared by every door
    that invents a name (`themes.new_name`): the name has to clear three
    things at once, because `themes.resolve()` matches three - the 27 layout
    names in the library, those names folded, and the 27 ids inside the
    files. That is measured rather than cautious: a copy put at a vendor's
    own name takes that layout out of reach by name *and* by id
    (`resolve(<that id>)` answers None afterwards), and a copy named
    `theme_07` beside the vendor's `Theme_07` leaves `--list-themes` showing
    two layouts while every spelling resolves to one file. A name is one
    path element, at most 64 characters, never one of the five drawn from
    `render.py`, and never ending in `.json`, `.bak` or `.tmp` - the
    suffixes this program writes beside a layout, so a layout called
    `x.json` would live at `x.json.json` while the `x.json` next to it is
    the layout named `x`; none of the 27 names here does, so that rule
    refuses nothing that exists. Only a copy of yours can be renamed, and
    the name box has no length cap on purpose: a box that truncated at 64
    would act on a name nobody typed, so the door refuses with its own
    sentence instead.

  * A rename changes one path and one key, and says both. The rows, the
    canvas and the background are untouched - the suite draws the layout
    before and after and requires the pixels to match - and it is a file
    act, not an edit: no undo step, and `dirty` exactly as it was. The `id`
    inside the file does change, to a fresh 13-digit number in the shape the
    vendor's own files use (their 27 fall inside 1 187 564 ms of one
    another, which is the stamp of an application saving a layout), because
    `--theme <id>` answers a layout by its number as well as its name and
    two files sharing one is a name space with a hole in it.
    `<name>.json.bak` travels with the copy so undoing the last save is
    still that save; a half-written `.tmp` is reported and left where it is.
    `save as` leaves the first file byte for byte and its id, and it is also
    how a layout drawn from `render.py` gets a file of its own: `gauge.json`
    is still refused as a second `theme_gauge`, while a copy named something
    else is yours and `--theme gauge` keeps drawing the code's picture.

  * Which file answers a name is asked, never remembered. The editor's state
    line, the layout menu's entries and numbers, and the revert sentence all
    read `themes.resolve()` on the current name - the same door `--list-
    themes`, the loop's log line and the control socket's `theme` command go
    through - because the three moments that move a file are exactly the
    moments a remembered word goes stale; after reverting a renamed copy the
    honest line is `no file answers this name`. The menu is rebuilt with its
    signals blocked, because selecting the entry for the layout already in
    memory would re-read the file and throw away what was typed since the
    last save. A loop re-resolves its name every frame, so a rename reaches
    it on the next frame, and if the name was yours alone then nothing
    answers it: `render.draw` falls back to `gauge`, measured pixel for
    pixel, which is why the note tells the reader to re-point the loop over
    the socket - while a fresh `--theme` of that dead name exits 2 with
    `themes.why_not`'s sentence. Two related corrections: the loop's own
    report called a layout in `~/.config/xigmalcd/themes` "the installed
    package", which is now `themes.origin()`'s wording, and
    `edit.Editor.open_theme` handed the editor the object `themes.load()`
    caches, so an unsaved keystroke reached what `scan()`, `pair()` and
    `describe()` read.

  * Gates for all of it, in the same twenty-two offline sections, no port
    opened by any of them.  The new checks take their shape from the files
    on this run - the envelope, the mandatory key sets, the id reuse, the
    `layerIndex` census - and hold the sentences in docs/gui.md,
    docs/limits.md and `edit.py`'s own comments to that count.  A figure
    that follows a face is printed with the machine it was read on, and the
    page labels whose machine each half of the pair belongs to; a run that
    matches neither half fails rather than loosening the claim.  The resize
    gates add the per-kind sweep - every drawn row, every edge its own rules
    call live, the arc identity re-checked after each accepted drag, and the
    box followed 1:1 wherever the geometry is declared - and the three `line`
    cases no shipped file carries, built in memory: a `rotate: 90` row with a
    `lineWidth`, a plain `lineWidth`, and a row whose extent belongs to a cap.
    The window half drives its own marks: the squares it paints are the ones
    the model named, each square grabs its own edge, the ask on the glass is
    that door's answer, the cursor says which way the row grows, and both the
    refusal and the overlap are said in the pane's note line.  The naming gates
    hold the rule to the library rather than to a list - 27 names, all distinct
    folded, and 27 ids, all distinct - measure a rename for changing exactly one
    key and moving no pixel, check that a fresh id clears every id on disk, and
    drive both buttons through the window: the menu's entries and its tooltip
    numbers recounting from `themes.scan()`, the name box keeping what was
    typed, nine refusals leaving every byte of the one copy on disk where it
    was, and the passage ending with the user root as empty as it found it.

  * Still not built, and said in the pages rather than left to be found: an
    editor drawing live readings (the pane draws the pinned fixture, which
    is the picture the documents describe), snapping a row to a grid or to a
    neighbour, and the half only glass can
    answer - `--no-clock` on the panel, the quiet-box re-measure `bars`
    owes, the pairing note seen on the panel, and the transcript stage 6
    asks for.

 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:30:41 +0200
xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.5) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The window shows its layout editor from the start.  It is the only
    place the window lists the 23 library layouts it can draw, and behind
    the `advanced controls` switch it stayed invisible until a reader
    found an unrelated checkbox.  Opening it still reads files and writes
    none.

  * What that switch folds is now nine controls in two boxes at the foot
    of the preview column, under the log: the panel's `conn 1` report and
    the guarded factory reset, word field and button with their box.
    `conn 1` answers with fourteen fields, and they took about ten lines
    of a 300 px column that was spending its height on clip rows.  This
    reverses what 0.4 documented - there the reset stayed on screen
    whatever the switch said.  What stands between a stray click and a
    factory reset is the word `reset` typed into the field and a button
    disabled until it is, and the suite now checks that guard in the
    folded state: hidden and disabled, then shown and still disabled,
    waking only when the word is typed.

  * A drop-down shows what it holds, box and list both.  The widest row
    the layout editor can put in its widget menu measures 247 px (`data
    Motherboard Temperature  at 452,136`, swept across all 28 layouts it
    can open) and 264 px on CI's runner, whose fonts differ.  A list
    opens at its box's width, so in 0.4's 300 px column every longer
    entry was cut off exactly where a reader is choosing between them.
    The popup's view gets the extra width instead (323 px for that row,
    340 px on the runner), which costs the column no pixel at all, and
    `_ed_box_readable` makes the closed box ask for its longest entry as
    well: an elided `data  Motherboard Tempe…` in the box that says
    which row you are editing is the same complaint one widget later.
    The box's own `minimumSizeHint` is still held under that width, so
    the column pays for the text and not for the widget.

  * The fresh window is 1440x1024 with the splitter asked for
    532/540/356, and those numbers are floors rather than a preference.
    (They were 1320x800 and 560/400/420 for most of this cycle, and that
    pair is what 0.5's own sizing rule replaced.)  Checked out at the
    `v0.4` tag and measured the same way, 0.4's
    1180 px window and 520/460/560 split came back as 520/300/352
    columns while the controls column's own contents measured 265 px
    folded and 375 px with the editor open: it grew a horizontal
    scrollbar *inside itself*, 89 px of its own contents off screen, and
    the library column overflowed by 7 px the same way.  Today those two
    columns' floors are 376 px and 373 px on the author's bench - their own
    contents, 362 px and 359 px, plus the width a vertical scrollbar
    reserves - and the suite builds a fresh window, reads the three columns
    back and fails if any of them has to scroll sideways to be read.

  * The layout editor shows its fields instead of hiding them behind a
    menu.  Picking a widget now lays out every field that kind carries,
    one row each: 10 rows for a `text` widget, 11 for `data`, 12 for
    `special`, 8 for `line`, 15 for `bar`, 18 for `arc` - counted across
    the 185 widgets in the 28 layouts the editor can open, from
    `edit.field_names` and `edit.extension_fields` rather than a table.
    The control per row is the model's answer as well: a menu where
    `edit.choices()` names a vocabulary, a checkbox for `bold`, a spinbox
    for `edit.number_fields()`, a colour row with a swatch and the
    dialog for `edit.COLOUR_FIELDS`, a text box for what is genuinely
    free text.  Rows reflow between one, two and three columns with the
    width the window has.  Fixing the gates turned up a defect the
    dropdown had been hiding: a row for a key the file never carried
    showed a placeholder 0 and wrote that 0 back on focus-out, inventing
    a key and an undo step; a box nobody touched no longer writes.

  * The window's floor is 1280x1024 and its fresh size is 1440x1024,
    with the splitter asked for 532/540/356 - the owner's rule, a
    1280x1024 desktop at minimum up to 3840x2160.  Each column's floor
    is now its own contents' width rather than a remembered constant,
    so no column can be dragged under what it holds and the sideways
    scrollbar 0.4 shipped is unreachable rather than merely avoided; the
    window clamps its floor to what the screen offers and logs the
    sentence when either clamp bites.  Measured at the sizes the rule
    names: one column of editor rows at 1280 (9 clip rows in view), two
    at 1440, three from 1919 up; 28 clip rows at 3840, with the preview
    still capped at 532 px because the 480 px frame is never scaled up.

  * Every measured figure a page quotes now carries the machine it was
    read on, and the suite holds it by that label - as a pair wherever the
    two machines differ.  At a 1280x1024 desktop the three columns' floors
    are 272/376/373 px here and 282/394/390 px on GitHub's runner; a fresh
    window reads its split back as 532/527/373 px and 532/510/390 px; the
    controls floor runs 376/400/568 px as the editor's rows take one, two
    and three columns here against 394/417/589 px there; the 18-row `arc`
    box stands 796 px in one column and 403 px in three against 723 and
    378, its grid asking 171/344/512 px against 180/361/533.  The gate
    reads every pair out of docs/gui.md and requires this run's numbers to
    be one half of it - twice now that has caught a runner digit that had
    gone stale.  What both machines agree on is stated once and asserted
    exactly (28 clip rows at 3840x2160); a width no two machines see alike
    is stated as a floor either clears, with the running machine's actual
    number printed beside the page's.

  * The editor's rows say what the row paints.  For a `text` or `data`
    widget the keys that choose the line - `content`, `source`, `xigma.format`,
    `fontFamily`, `fontSize`, `bold`, `anchor`, `autoFitMax`, `xigma.showIf` -
    print the string the row produces (`draws as "57°C"`), or why it produces
    none (`no reading for Fan 3 Name on this machine: the row is skipped`), or
    that the shape it was spelled with is `layout.VENDOR_FORMAT`'s rather than
    one measured off the vendor's picture.  The answer comes from
    `edit.drawn_text`, which walks the painter's rules in the painter's order,
    and it is proved by painting rather than by agreement: every text-bearing
    row of the 28 layouts is drawn twice, once as the file has it and once with
    that row replaced by a literal `text` widget holding the model's string, and
    the two frames must be byte-identical - 145 rows, 132 of them painting a
    line at this fixture here and 131 on CI's runner (no CJK face there for the
    vendor's `simsun` rows to open), 0 twins differing, 13 rows skipped by both
    doors here and 14 there, each with the reason said out loud.

  * `xigma.format` is refused when the value cannot be dressed in it, and a
    file that already carries one is survived.  `%q`, `%d %d`, `{}` and
    `{:,.0f}` all raise inside `format()` for the int this fixture gives for
    `Used RAM`: the model refuses each one, names the value it probed, and
    leaves `dirty` and the undo count exactly where they were; for a
    brace-style spelling it points at the form this field takes (`,.0f`), and
    only after checking that the suggestion paints.  In the other direction
    `layout.draw` records `format:<source> (ValueError)` in its skip list
    instead of raising, so a hand-edited file costs one row and says which.

  * The `fontFamily` row previews every family with the file this box resolves
    it to, drawn through the same Pillow call the painter makes, and names that
    file in the entry's tooltip.  A family whose globs match nothing here gets
    no preview and says so in three places, because such a row draws no ink at
    all: the painter puts `font:<family>` in its `missing` notes.  The other
    way to have no face - no `fontFamily` key in the row at all - is the same
    case, and there is no default face to fall back to: the report says `font:`
    with nothing after the colon.  Both branches are built by the suite, in
    memory, because no file in the library is in either state.

  * Two defects the new gates found, both in the editor's own loop.  A
    `format` the painter cannot apply was *accepted* by the model and raised
    inside `layout.draw`, which is a frame loop that stops on a value a row
    wrote.  And the note line followed keyboard focus, so changing the widget
    while a row kept focus left the line explaining a different row's field -
    the suite read a stale `draws as "57 C"` off a row that paints nothing.

  * The controls column's floor counts what a closed drop-down has to
    display, not just its own size hint: the widest entry any layout puts
    in that menu is 247 px, and the box now shows it instead of eliding
    it.  At the narrowest column this bench allows (425 px) the box is
    323 px around that 247 px entry and the popup's list is 323 px.
    docs/gui.md carries those as this bench's digits; the suite prints
    its own.

  * A column's floor reserves the width a vertical scrollbar takes off the
    viewport, and is re-measured once the splitter has settled.  Both
    halves were needed.  The floor was read from the contents' width, and
    a 411 px floor around 411 px of contents left a 397 px viewport with
    14 px of horizontal travel: the 0.4 defect, arriving through the rule
    written to prevent it.  And one pass cannot see its own effect,
    because raising a floor moves the splitter, and a narrower column
    re-flows the editor's rows into another number of columns - so the
    window settles the number over a bounded chain of queued passes.  The
    suite now fails on a pinned column that scrolls sideways at all, and
    on any pane whose floor sits under its own contents, at each of the
    five widths the sizing rule names.

  * A family spelled the file's way is folded once, and said.  The vendor's
    rows write `Arial` while `layout.FONTS` names `arial`, and
    `layout.font_for` lowercases before resolving, so the two are one face
    on the glass - which left the menu offering the row's own spelling and
    the model refusing it.  It no longer does: the row keeps the name
    `layout.FONTS` carries, the window says what was stored, and the suite
    draws both frames of a real vendor row and finds 0 px of difference.
    Nothing else folds - `source`, `showIf`, `name` and `anchor` are keys
    the painter matches exactly, so `cpu temperature` stays refused.

  * Gates for the words in the report and the faces in the menu.  The three
    pages that spell out what `--check-theme` reports have to name both skip
    prefixes the painter writes - `showIf:` for an unmet dependency,
    `format:` for a shape the value cannot be dressed in - and may not name a
    prefix no code path appends; that set is read out of `layout.py` itself
    rather than listed here.  The same page's count of the families the
    library names is now counted from the library: it said four for a set of
    files that name three (`arial` `simsun` `verdana`), out of the five names
    `layout.FONTS` answers.

  * Gates for the layout itself, in the same twenty-two offline sections.
    A fresh window is checked against its own contents: no column
    narrower than what it holds, no horizontal scrollbar in any of the
    three.  The controls pane is then pinned to its own floor, which is
    the narrowest column the window allows - no longer the 300 px 0.4
    handed it - and the width is read back before anything is claimed
    about it, because a splitter request shares out spare width and the
    first version of that check measured a 340 px column while believing
    it had pinned 300.
    The switch's note, its tooltip and docs/gui.md are held to one list
    of what folds - nine controls in two boxes, and the layout editor
    deliberately not among them.  The window and split figures
    docs/gui.md, docs/theme-editor.md and docs/tools.md quote are read
    back off the code and the live widgets, and so is the number of clip
    rows the README's capture frame puts in view.  Where a figure follows
    the machine's fonts the page states a labelled pair, and the gate
    holds this run to one half of it: the three columns' floors are
    272/376/373 px here and 282/394/390 px on CI's runner, and a run
    that matches neither half fails rather than loosening the gate.

  * The README's advanced-mode live capture no longer shows this panel's
    serial number.  The `conn 1` reply the window prints names it, and the
    capture published on 2026-11-08 kept it as pixels: legible to an OCR
    pass, invisible to a grep of the staged diff, which is the check this
    project had been trusting for it.  Painted out with the same
    `<redacted>` wording docs/gui.md uses for that field, at the face,
    size and colour the window itself used.  `tools/selftest.sh` now
    searches both halves of the serial through every published text file
    and every picture docs/packaging.md accounts for, by OCR where
    `tesseract` is installed and saying so where it is not;
    docs/limits.md and docs/packaging.md keep the account, including that
    the published history still holds the file as it was - rewriting it
    would move tagged releases, which is the owner's call, not a quiet
    edit.

 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:27:56 +0200
xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The vendor's own layouts are pictures this program can draw.  `--theme
    Theme_07`, `{"cmd":"theme","value":"Theme_07"}` over the control socket,
    and the window's layout editor all name one file.  23 of ALPHA's 27
    layouts are selectable; the four 320x240 ones are refused on their own
    `ratio` field rather than stretched onto a 480x480 glass, and
    `--list-themes` prints the split with the reason beside it.
    `--check-theme NAME` prints what every widget of one layout would do -
    painted, skipped because no reading answers its `source`, or skipped
    because a font face is not installed - and the counts account for every
    widget in the file, so nothing vanishes uncounted.  No layout file is
    ever sent to the panel: it is drawn here and uploaded as the same `.osd`
    frame a picture of `alpha` already is.

  * All five of this project's own themes now export to that file format at
    zero differing pixels (`--export-theme NAME`), which is what the format
    is for.  Getting there meant naming two things the vendor's renderer
    never had to write down: `xigma.arcPaint`, because one arc painted with
    the vendor's convention is a different picture from the same arc painted
    with ours (measured both ways in the suite), and `xigma.colourStops`,
    because `gauge`'s ramp is a colour rule rather than a gradient somebody
    remembered.  `--list-specials` prints the five composite rows
    (`fan-figures`, `temp-values`, `temp-labels`, `dual-status`,
    `clock-readings`) that no single vendor widget stands for.

  * `bars` lays out ten tachometer slots on every box instead of compacting
    the list to whatever one machine reports, so a layout file can say where
    a row goes; the two bar conventions it needed are held to the functions
    they name.  The readings that go behind those rows were checked against
    what the drawing prints, row for row.

  * A theme you edit is a file, and a file you write shadows the vendor's.
    `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xigmalcd/themes` is read before the checkout and
    before `/usr/share/xigmalcd/themes/alpha`, so your copy wins without
    anyone unpacking a package, and `--list-themes` says which root
    answered.  The two vendor trees are never written, and the five
    drawn-from-code names cannot be saved as files: a `gauge.json` would be
    a second source of truth for `render.theme_gauge`, which is the drift
    the export design exists to avoid.  `--check-theme gauge` says that in
    words.

  * The window's layout editor.  Pick one of the 23 selectable layouts and
    it is drawn by the same code the loop uses, from a pinned fixture, so
    the picture does not move under your cursor while you edit it; point at
    a row's ink box to select it, drag it, nudge it with the arrow keys.
    Applying is live to the preview only - nothing about an edit reaches the
    panel except through the buttons that already sent frames, and the
    guarded factory reset is still never folded away.

  * A layout names its own background, and the window follows it.  Every
    selectable layout declares a clip in `background.mediaFile`; choosing
    the layout puts that clip under the preview and marks its row, and the
    line under the picture says which file it chose and from where.  It is a
    preview rule, not a wire rule: the upload button is still the only thing
    that sends a file to the panel.  One click on another row makes the
    background yours, and from then on no layout can walk over it until the
    `Use this layout's own clip` button hands it back.  That refusal is now
    said in the log as well as in the line under the picture: a preview that
    will not move, with nothing else about the window changed, is otherwise
    indistinguishable from a broken one.

  * `media_roots()` gained the vendor's theme library, so a bare clip name
    is looked up in four roots instead of two: `$XIGMA_MEDIA`, this
    checkout's `media/`, `/usr/share/xigmalcd/media`, then the library - the
    checkout's `themes/alpha/clips`, then the copy
    `xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes` installs.  That is what makes `--mp4
    Theme_07.mp4` mean the file `--list-themes` printed beside that layout:
    23 of the 23 resolve, where 2 of 23 did before.  The media roots still
    come first, so the session default is `media/Theme_25.mp4` on every box,
    and no file is uploaded that a flag did not ask for.

  * The two doors made to agree about a frame.  A generated layout and the
    code drawing it came from are compared at one pinned reading dict and
    one pinned instant - 15 of the 19 sources a layout asks for are answered
    by that one dict, and the reader's own text colour reaches a generated
    file through declared skin roles (`render.SKIN`, `themes.with_ink`)
    rather than an `ink` argument, which is what lets the ink comparisons
    land at zero differing pixels at all.

  * `--bg black` is a canvas, not a promise about the finished frame, and
    the documents said the promise.  For a layout theme the plate is
    composited *under* the finished layer, so every pixel comes out at alpha
    255 and the panel's stored background is covered.  For three of the five
    drawn themes (`alpha`, `dual`, `gauge`) it is not: a `theme_*` body
    opens its image on the canvas, PIL replaces a translucent fill instead
    of blending it, and those themes keep letting the stored background
    through their own ring tracks and plates.  Which themes leak is derived
    from the exports' colour keys by the suite, which prints the pixel
    counts beside the machine instead of quoting them.

  * `--no-clock` does what it says on the theme that is mostly a clock.
    `theme_clock` took a `clock` argument and never read it, so the flag
    moved zero pixels; it now drops the big time line and leaves the date
    and the readings line where they are.  That is a frame change on the
    glass for anyone using the flag - every frame drawn *without* it is
    byte-identical to before, and the suite measures that rather than
    asserting it.

  * Two questions that used to be argued are now measured by a tool.
    `--print-theme-fields` prints the field census over the vendor's files
    and `layout.py`'s own source: 33 keys across 152 widgets, 22 of them
    read, 11 carried by the vendor's files and never read, 4 read with no
    vendor example.  `tools/knobmap.py` answers, option by option, what a
    layout file can do in place of a `theme_*` body: 28 of the 37 (theme,
    option) pairs land at zero differing pixels and 9 do not, and the suite
    holds the floor above 900 px on any machine - which is the recorded
    reason the five stay drawn from code.

  * An installed library is read back now, not only hashed.  With the themes
    package present, `debian/tests/smoke` runs `themes.scan()` against the
    installed tree, paints every selectable layout through `themes.paint()`
    from one pinned dict at one pinned instant, holds the per-layout
    accounting identity, agrees with `--list-themes` on the same split, and
    resolves each layout's declared clip through the loop's own name lookup.
    The themes `.deb` is built, uploaded and installed on every CI run
    rather than only on a tag: before that, the whole section printed
    SKIPPED on a push and the figures rested on one bench.

  * The five themes this repository draws from `render.py` pair with a
    background too. `themes.CODE_CLIPS` gives each of them the backdrop its
    own published picture is composited over - `alpha` with Theme_25.mp4,
    `gauge` with 3.mp4, `dual` with Theme_30.mp4, `clock` with Theme_07.mp4,
    `bars` with theme_52.mp4 - so choosing one in the window's layout dropdown
    moves the preview the way choosing a vendor page does: 5 of 5 through the
    theme picker and 5 of 5 through the dropdown's export entries, each with
    its library row marked.  That set is not typed in twice.  The suite reads
    the README's `<theme>-<clip>.mp4` names back off the written files, counts
    a candidate only when its clip part resolves as a clip this box holds -
    which is how the amber-ink figure drops out, by not resolving rather than
    by hand - and fails if the module's table disagrees with the derivation.
    Four of the five backdrops measure dark and need no plate; `clock`'s is
    bright, so the scrim arrives with it through the same luma rule as
    everywhere else.

  * The layout dropdown now chooses its own background.  A vendor layout
    declares one clip in its own file, and picking the layout puts that clip
    under the preview and marks its row - 23 of 23 on this bench, and the
    suite drives every one of them.  The old rule was the other way round:
    clicking a clip row set `clip_customized`, and from then on no layout
    could move the picture, which is how a window came to look like it was
    ignoring its own pairing for a reason nobody could see.  The opt-out is
    now a box you can see - *keep my background*, stored as `keep_my_clip` -
    and a hand is the only thing that ticks it.  Clicking a row says what the
    pick costs instead of setting a flag; the button takes the layout's clip
    back and unticks the box; unticking moves the picture at once rather than
    waiting for the next layout change.  `clip_customized` is still read once,
    honoured as a ticked box so an upgrade moves nothing under anybody,
    announced in the log before the window does anything else, and gone from
    the next save.

  * A test section that wrote the author's session file, and the rule it
    taught.  The suite's GUI section set `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=$cfgtmp` from a
    variable assigned 600 lines earlier in a different section, so run on its
    own that expanded to nothing and every window in it wrote
    `~/.config/xigmaui/config.json`: device `/dev/xigma-no-such-port`,
    `preview_fps 1.0`, and `clip_customized` true, which is the flag that
    stops the window following any layout's own background - so the next
    `python3 -m xigmaui` by hand opened on a clip nobody had chosen.  That
    section now makes its own settings root and asserts the file it writes is
    inside it and not in a home directory; and `Window` gained
    `save_settings`, which defaults to `restore`, because a window told to
    ignore a session file is not a window that should replace one.  `--fresh`
    still writes, since its help promises to overwrite, and a window that
    declines says so once in its own log.

  * Gates for the things that had quietly drifted.  `tools/selftest.sh`,
    twenty-two offline sections, now reads the width of debian/changelog (a
    line over 80 columns is a lintian *warning*, which fails the .deb job
    while the python suite stays green), re-derives the clip census from the
    files this repository tracks (46 mp4 files, 40 distinct contents, 6
    copies, 3 of them renamed) instead of quoting an audit of one machine's
    mounted partitions, holds the pairing rule to the layout files
    themselves, and re-prints every sentence in the documents that quotes a
    number a tool prints - including the numbers in docs/tools.md, two of
    which had gone stale a day after they were written.

